Depends on: the Git::Raw API card.
Evidence (with the API fixed in a scratch subclass): the history of Intro#Alpha listed "v2, unrelated page, v1"; the middle commit only touched other.md. A client cannot pick a restore point from that.

Fix: list only commits in which the section's text (heading line + body) differs from its text in the first parent, or the section is new there. Output format unchanged.

Tests: opt-in real-Git::Raw file: a commit touching only another page, and one touching only another section of the same page, are not listed.

From k30 (WB): History parses pages raw, without Document's CRLF->LF step, so on a CRLF page with an empty '# ' heading its heading_paths differ from Document's (edge case). When touching History's section lookup, parse the same normalised text Document does.

tests: t/49-git-section-history.t (opt-in, same BEGIN isolation as t/42; 5 commits via tools: create page, create other page, change Beta, change Alpha x2). Expects Intro#Alpha -> [c5,c4,c1] newest first, Intro#Beta -> [c3,c1], keys unchanged. Red on HEAD: 3 assertions (Alpha list has 5 entries, count, Beta list).

WA: get_section_commits lists a commit only when the section is new there or its text (heading line + text above its trailing blank lines — the span a restore brings back) differs from the first parent's; output unchanged. Gap-only commits not listed (restoring them would change nothing). t/49 passes with Git::Raw 0.90 and fails on HEAD+k24-only on the listing checks; t/42 still passes. Code in History.pm on top of k24 (commit after k24).

